(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-cam-nutrition-exam2-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CAM/Nutrition Exam 2 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which populations does the lecture say require particular caution with herbal dosing?","choices":["Athletes, shift workers, and frequent travellers","Pregnancy, lactation, children, the elderly","Only those patients over the age of eighty","Only patients taking more than five prescription drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture advises avoiding use in contemplated pregnancy and lactation, extreme care in dosing children with weight-based doses, and warns about polypharmacy in the elderly. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the lecture give as the educational path to a Doctor of Chiropractic degree?","choices":["A high school diploma followed by a four-year apprenticeship","A master's degree followed by a two-year residency","A bachelor's degree, then two semesters of coursework and one clinical year","A bachelor's degree, six didactic and four clinical semesters, then boards"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The degree takes three and a half to four years, with an average load of 25 to 27 credits. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which herbal preparation does the lecture describe as containing alcohol?","choices":["Tea pills","Capsules","Tinctures","Teas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes tinctures as rapidly absorbed and containing alcohol. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which of the following does the lecture list among chiropractic diagnostics?","choices":["Blood chemistry and inflammatory markers","Posture analysis, both stationary and walking","Nerve conduction velocity as a routine first test","Genetic testing for connective tissue disorders"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The list also includes history, inspection, palpation, orthopaedic and neurologic testing, imaging, and body scans. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Under the Fair Housing Act, what may a landlord NOT do when a tenant has a qualifying emotional support animal with a letter?","choices":["Charge a pet fee, restrict breed, deny housing, or evict","Ask whether the animal is required because of a disability","Require the animal to be leashed in common areas","Request a copy of the emotional support animal letter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these four things a landlord is not allowed to do. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which animal, besides dogs, must entities allow as a reasonable modification under the Americans with Disabilities Act?","choices":["Cats, if recommended by a physician","Rabbits, if housebroken","Capuchin monkeys, if professionally trained","Miniature horses, if they have been trained"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states service animals are limited to dogs, but entities must make reasonable modifications to allow individuals with disabilities to use miniature horses if they have been trained. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does the lecture define a chiropractic adjustment?","choices":["A deep soft tissue massage of the paraspinal muscles","A sustained low-velocity stretch held for several minutes","A high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust, usually given by hand","A rhythmic oscillation applied through a mechanical table only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is directed at articulations of the spine or the extremities. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish visualization from guided imagery?","choices":["Visualization always requires supervision by a trained therapist, whereas guided imagery is by definition entirely self-directed by the patient","Visualization is used only for pain, whereas guided imagery is used only for anxiety","Visualization refers to seeing images in the mind's eye, whereas guided imagery uses one or more senses to create a detailed mental experience","Visualization occurs during sleep, whereas guided imagery occurs while fully awake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines visualization as \"seeing\" images in the mind's eye and guided imagery as engaging one or more senses to build a detailed mental experience. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which actions does the lecture attribute to a seizure response dog?","choices":["Interrupting hand flapping with a discreet, unobtrusive nudge","Predicting seizures several hours in advance by scent alone","Administering rescue medication directly to the handler","Barking to alert a caregiver, pressing alarms, and breaking a fall"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists all four of these actions for a seizure response dog. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the legal nature of a medical marijuana authorisation in Florida, as described in the lecture?","choices":["A recommendation from a physician, not a prescription, for home use only","A prescription that may be filled at any retail pharmacy","A federal waiver issued by the Drug Enforcement Administration","An over-the-counter authorisation requiring no clinician involvement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes it as a recommendation from a physician rather than a prescription, allowed for home use only, approved by the state while still illegal federally. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which set of attributes does the lecture assign to yin?","choices":["Hypoactive, excited, quiet, bright, summer","Hyperactive, inhibited, loud, dark, winter","Hyperactive, excited, loud, bright, red","Hypoactive, inhibited, quiet, pale, winter"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also pairs yin with the moon and with 'female'. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does the lecture warn against combining vitamin K, fish oil, and 'blood moving' herbs with warfarin?","choices":["They inactivate warfarin, causing clot formation and stroke","They increase the anticoagulant effect of warfarin, leading to bleeding","They cause warfarin to accumulate in fat stores and act unpredictably","They block warfarin absorption when taken at the same time of day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states these can increase warfarin's anticoagulant effect and cause bleeding. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are chiropractors trained to locate, and what is the consequence of what they find?","choices":["Joint effusions, which cause swelling and stiffness","Misaligned vertebrae, which result in nerve pressure","Disc degeneration, which causes loss of height","Muscle spasm, which causes referred pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Techniques then eliminate that nerve pressure by restoring proper joint alignment and motion. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which of the following is one of the five domains of complementary and alternative medicine presented in the lecture?","choices":["Manipulative and body-based practices","Pharmacologic and surgical interventions","Diagnostic imaging and laboratory testing","Emergency and critical care services"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The five domains are whole medical systems, mind-body medicine, biologically based practices, manipulative and body-based practices, and energy therapies. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"According to the JAMA 2015 recommendations quoted in the lecture, an appropriate medical marijuana candidate should have which of the following?","choices":["A terminal diagnosis confirmed by one single physician","Any chronic condition, provided the patient requests a certification","A debilitating condition after failed first-line, second-line and cannabinoid trials","Failure of one first-line agent, with no further requirement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these together with no active substance abuse, psychotic disorder, or unstable mood or anxiety disorder. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Who founded the techniques of modern Swedish massage?","choices":["Per Ling, a Swedish physician of the early 19th century","Albert Hoffa, working in the late 19th century","Gertrude Beard, working in the 20th century","James Mennell, working in the early 20th century"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His techniques were incorporated with French massage techniques as well. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What does the lecture mean by saying horses mirror the emotions of those around them?","choices":["Horses imitate the handler's physical movements exactly","A calm handler produces a calm horse","Horses become aggressive when handled by strangers","Horses only respond to other horses' emotional states"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives the example directly as calm equals calm, in the context of horses mirroring the emotions of those around them. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which reason does the lecture give for using acupuncture?","choices":["It is a different way of looking at the patient's collection of symptoms","It removes any need for referral on to other treating clinicians","It replaces the need to reach a diagnosis before treating","It works faster than every available pharmacologic option"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also cites fewer side effects, cost effectiveness, recognising the limits of allopathic medicine, and an integrative approach. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"The lecture raises a specific safety concern about Ayurvedic preparations. What is it?","choices":["They are frequently contaminated with prescription antibiotics during manufacturing","Some include metals, minerals, or gems, and testing has found high levels of lead and mercury","They must be refrigerated continuously or they lose all therapeutic activity","They are dangerous only when combined with traditional Chinese herbal products"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes that some Ayurvedic preparations include metals, minerals, or gems, that one in four supplements tested had high lead levels and almost half had high mercury levels, and that the Food and Drug Administration warns some products may be potentially harmful. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which description matches transcendental meditation as the lecture presents it?","choices":["Systematically tensing and relaxing muscle groups throughout the body","Being intensely aware of what one senses and feels in the moment without interpretation or judgment","Silently repeating a mantra, traditionally practiced for twenty minutes twice daily","Using electronic monitoring to display physiologic function in real time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes transcendental meditation as silently repeating a mantra, traditionally for twenty minutes twice daily, studied for stress, anxiety, and cardiovascular health. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which three amino acids combine to form glutathione, as given in the lecture?","choices":["Cysteine, glycine, and glutamine","Methionine, lysine, and arginine","Taurine, serine, and alanine","Tryptophan, tyrosine, and valine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes glutathione as a combination of cysteine, glycine and glutamine, with its detoxifying power in the sulfur. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"In what year did the National Institutes of Health formally recognise the effectiveness of acupuncture, according to the lecture's timeline?","choices":["1996","1997","2002","1992"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's timeline places formal recognition of effectiveness in 1997. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Must a hospital allow a patient with a disability to keep a service animal in the room?","choices":["Generally yes, anywhere patients and the public may go","Only if the animal remains crated at all times","No, because infection control always takes precedence","Only if staff cannot provide the same service the animal provides"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states service animals must be allowed in patient rooms and anywhere else the public and patients are allowed to go, and cannot be excluded on the grounds that staff can provide the same services. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How do onset and duration compare between oral and inhaled medical marijuana?","choices":["Oral onset 60 to 90 seconds lasting 2 hours; inhaled onset 10 to 30 minutes lasting 6 to 8 hours","Oral onset 10 to 30 minutes lasting 6 to 8 hours; inhaled onset 60 to 90 seconds lasting 2 hours","Both routes have an onset of about 10 to 30 minutes and last 6 to 8 hours","Oral onset 2 hours lasting 60 to 90 minutes; inhaled onset 30 seconds lasting 10 hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives oral onset as 10 to 30 minutes with effect lasting 6 to 8 hours, and inhalation onset as 60 to 90 seconds lasting 2 hours. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What belief underlies reflexology as the lecture describes it?","choices":["That spinal alignment determines the function of every peripheral joint","That specific areas of the feet, hands, or ears correspond to specific organs or body systems","That repeated dilution of a remedy increases its therapeutic potency","That the balance among three fundamental body energies is what determines overall health"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that reflexology involves applying pressure to specific areas of the feet, hands, or ears, which practitioners believe correspond to particular organs or body systems. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"According to the lecture, which feature most clearly distinguishes integrative medicine from simply using a complementary therapy?","choices":["It requires the patient to discontinue all prescription medications first","It restricts care exclusively to those therapies that have already been formally reviewed and approved by the Food and Drug Administration","It is limited to practices that originated outside of Western medicine","It is a patient-centered, whole-person approach that deliberately combines conventional care with evidence-based complementary therapies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes integrative medicine as patient-centered and whole-person, combining conventional medicine with evidence-based complementary therapies while emphasizing wellness, prevention, and the therapeutic relationship. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which set of practices does the lecture give as common within traditional Chinese medicine?","choices":["Hydrotherapy, exercise therapy, nutritional supplementation, and mind-body therapy","Chelation infusion, botanical extraction, specialized diets, and supplement regimens","Progressive relaxation, guided imagery, hypnotherapy, and mantra repetition","Acupuncture, herbal medicine, dietary therapy, massage, tai chi, and qigong"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these as the common practices of traditional Chinese medicine, which originated in China and evolved over thousands of years. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which of the following does the lecture list as an outright contraindication rather than a caution?","choices":["Local infection","Diabetes","Needle phobia","Warfarin use and bleeding disorders"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture marks local infection as a contraindication, while the others on its list are cautions. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which therapies does the lecture name as examples that aim to manipulate proposed energy fields?","choices":["Homeopathy, naturopathy, and Ayurvedic dosha balancing techniques","Chiropractic, osteopathic manipulation, and therapeutic massage technique","Chelation, botanical medicine, and specialized therapeutic dietary patterns","Reiki, therapeutic touch, and acupuncture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names Reiki, therapeutic touch, and acupuncture as therapies that aim to manipulate proposed energy fields to promote healing. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What does the lecture conclude about the evidence supporting cupping?","choices":["Evidence is strong and supports cupping as a first-line treatment for back pain","Evidence shows benefit only when cupping is combined with spinal manipulation","Evidence is limited, and the benefits that are seen appear to be primarily short term","Evidence demonstrates no measurable effect on local blood flow in any study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that evidence for cupping is limited and that benefits appear to be primarily short term, while noting it is commonly used for neck and low back pain. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about how sensitive horses are to human presentation?","choices":["They are exquisitely sensitive to body language and nonverbal cues","They respond mainly to tone of voice rather than body language","They habituate quickly and stop responding to handler cues","They respond only after months of familiarity with a handler"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states horses are exquisitely sensitive to how we present ourselves and highly responsive to human body language and nonverbal cues. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How does the Americans with Disabilities Act define a service animal?","choices":["A dog individually trained to do work for a person with a disability","Any animal that provides comfort to a person with a disability","A dog or cat certified by a licensed mental health professional","Any trained animal accepted by the establishment being entered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this definition and notes the disability may be physical, sensory, psychiatric, intellectual or other mental disability. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What did research show about the cardiovascular effects once attributed to deep or vigorous massage?","choices":["It confirmed a large rise in arterial blood flow","It failed to support them, showing no rise in output or blood flow","It confirmed a sustained increase in cardiac output","It showed a permanent reduction in resting heart rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Massage can, however, decrease heart rate, breathing rate and blood pressure when used for relaxation. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What distinguishes effleurage performed with the palm from effleurage performed with the fingertips?","choices":["The palm stimulates deep tissue, the fingertips sensory nerves","The palm is used only on limbs and the fingertips only on the back","The palm removes dead skin cells while the fingertips do not","The palm is used without oil while the fingertips require it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture distinguishes them in exactly this way. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How does Traditional Chinese Medicine define illness, according to the lecture?","choices":["An invasion of the body by an external organism","A structural abnormality of an organ requiring correction","A disturbance of Qi and disharmony between yin and yang","An accumulation of waste products in the tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment therefore aims to balance yin and yang, and preventing illness is paramount. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which species does the lecture say may serve as an emotional support animal?","choices":["Dogs only, according to current federal rules","Dogs and miniature horses only, by regulation","Usually a cat or dog, sometimes a turtle or rabbit","Any animal at all, except for reptiles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states an emotional support animal is usually a cat or dog but may be a turtle, horse or rabbit. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which psychological effects does the lecture attribute to massage?","choices":["Reduced anxiety, depression and stress, with better compliance","Increased dependence on the clinician","Improved sleep quality specifically","Reduced appetite and improved mood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture attributes these to the one-on-one nature of treatment. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which of the following does the lecture name as a style of Tai Chi?","choices":["Yang","Rolfing","Shiatsu","Tui Na"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists Yang, Chen, Wu, Sun, and Fu styles. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"How does the American Hippotherapy Association define hippotherapy?","choices":["A recreational riding programme for people with disabilities","A mental health treatment delivered by a therapist alongside an equine specialist","A physical, occupational, or speech therapy treatment strategy that utilises equine movement","An experiential learning approach that develops life skills"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives the American Hippotherapy Association definition as a physical, occupational or speech therapy treatment strategy utilising equine movement. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why does the lecture describe horses as suited to therapy work?","choices":["They are predators, so they read human aggression accurately","They are solitary animals, so they bond with only a single person","They are domesticated to the point of ignoring our body language","Sentient prey animals, socially aware, and they mirror our emotions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes horses as sentient beings that perceive and feel, prey animals highly aware of their surroundings, social and herd animals that mirror emotions, establish boundaries, and seek a leader they can trust. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the stated long-term goal of biofeedback training?","choices":["To identify which medications the patient can safely discontinue","To teach patients to self-regulate without reliance on monitoring equipment","To produce a permanent change in resting brain anatomy","To establish the patient's dominant dosha before treatment planning"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that through repeated practice and feedback patients learn to modify physiologic functions, with the goal of self-regulation without relying on the monitoring equipment. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How many acupuncture points does the lecture give for the meridian system?","choices":["About 250 points plus extra points","361 points plus extra points","About 500 points plus extra points","About 100 points plus extra points"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture adds that the points have numbers and names standardized by the World Health Organization. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why does the lecture list diabetes among the cautions for acupuncture?","choices":["Because needles interfere with insulin absorption","Because of neuropathy","Because treatment raises blood glucose","Because of the risk of hypoglycaemia during treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives neuropathy as the reason diabetes is a caution. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How do horses contribute to self-awareness in equine-assisted psychotherapy?","choices":["By responding only to verbal commands, which teaches clear speech","By mirroring and feeding back what they observe in our behaviour","By being unresponsive, which forces the client to self-reflect","By competing with the client, which builds resilience"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes horses offering feedback and mirroring, being keen observers, vigilant and environmentally aware, and sensitive to movement and nonverbal cues, which promotes self-awareness. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How many licensed chiropractors and accredited degree programmes does the lecture give for the United States today?","choices":["Over 100,000 chiropractors and 30 accredited programmes","Over 25,000 chiropractors and 12 accredited programmes","Over 70,000 chiropractors and 19 accredited programmes","Over 38,000 chiropractors and 47 accredited programmes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture adds that the profession is regulated in all states. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the duration of Tai Chi practice?","choices":["Benefits are unrelated to how long it is practised","A single session is enough to gain the benefits","Twelve-week classes help, but long-term continuation prolongs the benefits","Benefits appear only after several years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture recommends developing a routine. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say horses are well suited to therapeutic work?","choices":["Because they are predators, and so respond to dominance from the handler","Because they are solitary animals and bond to a single person","Because they are sentient prey animals that mirror emotions and read nonverbal cues","Because they are indifferent to human body language, so sessions stay neutral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes them as highly aware of their surroundings and exquisitely sensitive to how we present ourselves. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which form of coenzyme Q10 does the lecture describe as much better absorbed?","choices":["Ubiquinone, the oxidised form","Ubiquinol, the reduced form","Both are absorbed identically","Neither is absorbed orally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states ubiquinol, the reduced form, absorbs up to eight times better than ubiquinone and remains in the blood far longer. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How does the lecture explain the purpose of cupping relative to massage?","choices":["Cupping replaces massage entirely within Traditional Chinese Medicine practice","Massage is used only once a course of cupping has failed","Massage can move toxins, but where build-up is significant, massage alone is not enough","Cupping is used only for cosmetic and appearance purposes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls cupping a way of clearing the drain, warming the meridians and dispelling stagnation. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about petrissage and neuromuscular excitability?","choices":["It decreases excitability, but only during the massage itself","It decreases excitability throughout the body for 24 hours","It increases excitability for several hours afterwards","It has no measurable effect on excitability"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture confines the effect both in time and in location. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is naturopathy founded on, and where does it direct its attention?","choices":["The measurement of physiologic signals, directing attention toward learned self-regulation","The insertion of needles at defined points, directing attention toward the flow of the vital force","The healing power of nature, directing attention toward the cause of disease rather than the symptoms alone","The balance of opposing forces, directing attention toward harmony with the external environment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes naturopathy as founded on the healing power of nature, emphasizing prevention, a healthy lifestyle, treatment of the whole person, and finding the cause of disease rather than just treating symptoms. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"With which technique does the lecture say each session usually begins and ends?","choices":["Petrissage","Effleurage","Friction","Tapotement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes it as a smooth gliding technique usually performed with oil or lotion. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which conditions does the lecture list as reasons a patient should not use echinacea?","choices":["Diabetes, hypoglycemia, or use of blood sugar–altering drugs","Liver disorder or symptoms such as dark urine and jaundice","Autoimmune disease, mood disorder, asthma, cancer, or seizures","Shellfish allergy, sulfa allergy, or pineapple allergy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these conditions as reasons not to use echinacea, while noting its interaction risk with medications is low. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"By which mechanisms does the lecture say massage reduces pain?","choices":["By easing swelling and spasm, and by increasing blood flow","By blocking nerve conduction in the affected limb","By releasing endorphins and enkephalins centrally","By reducing systemic inflammatory markers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It also activates sensory nerves, which inhibits pain. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which factors does the lecture say affect the potency of an herbal product?","choices":["Only the particular plant species used","Climate, soil, growing and storage conditions, and batch","Only the extraction solvent used in manufacturing","Only the dose that is printed on the label"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists climate, soil conditions, location grown, harvested and stored, and batch differences as determinants of potency. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What distinguishes the flying cup technique?","choices":["The cup is heated and then applied without creating any suction","The cup is left in one place for the whole of the treatment","The cup is moved slowly along a meridian while still attached","The cup is quickly removed and replaced several times on the same area"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this as a way to relieve muscle tension and release body toxins. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which body systems do manipulative and body-based practices target, according to the lecture?","choices":["The endocrine glands together with the hormones they release into the circulation","The proposed biofields believed to surround the body","The central and peripheral nervous system pathways","The musculoskeletal system and soft tissues, including bones, joints, muscles, and connective tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines this domain by its focus on the musculoskeletal system and soft tissues, using hands-on techniques to improve function, reduce pain, and promote well-being. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Under the 2023 Florida limits, what is the daily dose amount permitted for edibles?","choices":["190 mg THC","60 mg THC","195 mg THC","350 mg THC"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's dispensing table gives edibles a daily dose amount of 60 mg THC and a 70-day supply limit of 4,200 mg THC. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise delta-9 tetrahydrocannabinol at the cannabinoid receptors?","choices":["A full agonist at CB2 with no CB1 activity","A competitive antagonist at CB1, which explains its psychoactivity","A partial agonist at both CB1 and CB2, with higher affinity for CB1","An inverse agonist at CB2 that reduces inflammation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states delta-9 THC is a partial agonist of both CB1 and CB2 receptors but has higher affinity for CB1. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are meridians, as the lecture defines them?","choices":["The peripheral nerves along which sensation travels","The lymphatic channels that drain each organ","Paths through which Qi travels around the body","The muscle planes used to locate trigger points"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stimulating points on a meridian affects the flow of Qi and the disease process or imbalance. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
